Latent trauma metric stratification of CTQ
CTQ subscales exhibit high mutual inter-correlation due to shared response variance. Modeling observed subscale scores as independent predictors introduces multicollinearity and leads to overestimating trauma burden when multiple subscales are elevated simultaneously (analogous to multiple motion sensors triggering from a single event). To resolve this, we employed a Bayesian implicit latent variable framework, modeling five observed CTQ subscale indicators (\(\mathbf{y}_{\text{EA}}, \mathbf{y}_{\text{PA}}, \mathbf{y}_{\text{SA}}, \mathbf{y}_{\text{EN}}, \mathbf{y}_{\text{PN}}\)) as expressions of two orthogonal/correlated latent factors: Abuse (\(c_{1,i}\)) and Neglect (\(c_{2,i}\)).Latent factor scores (\(c_{1}, c_{2}\)) represent standardized, continuous latent trait dimensions. Subjects were categorized into trauma severity tiers based on their posterior latent metric distributions:
Low Trauma Burden: Latent factor score \(\le -0.75\)
Medium Trauma Burden: \(-0.75 &lt; \text{Latent factor score} &lt; 0.75\)
High Trauma Burden: Latent factor score \(\ge 0.75\)
By evaluating the dynamic baseline rate parameter \(\lambda_{\text{subj}}\) on these latent continuum vectors (\(\gamma_{\text{abuse}} \cdot c_{1,i} + \gamma_{\text{neglect}} \cdot c_{2,i}\)), the model isolates the underlying dimensional impact of childhood trauma while fully accounting for measurement uncertainty.







S_Figure 8: Latent abuse–neglect correlation. The red line shows the posterior median and the dashed lines display the 95% credibility interval.




Because \(c_{1,i}\) and \(c_{2,i}\) are full posterior draws (not point estimates), each subject gets a posterior distribution over tiers rather than a single label.







